examples.question_text:
Decide whether each graph is a tree. Give a reason. Crossing edges meet only at marked vertices.

Graph 1:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\coordinate (P) at (0,2);
\coordinate (Q) at (4,2);
\coordinate (R) at (0,0);
\coordinate (S) at (4,0);
\draw (P)--(Q)--(R);
\draw (P)--(S);
\foreach \v/\pos in {P/above left,Q/above right,R/below left,S/below right}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);\path (\v) node[\pos] {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Graph 2:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\coordinate (P) at (0,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (1,2);
\coordinate (R) at (2,0);
\coordinate (S) at (3.5,2);
\coordinate (T) at (3.5,0);
\draw (P)--(Q)--(R)--(P);
\draw (S)--(T);
\foreach \v/\pos in {P/below left,Q/above,R/below right,S/above,T/below}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);\path (\v) node[\pos] {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Draw two different spanning trees of this network. It includes a loop at $P$ and two edges between $Q$ and $R$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\coordinate (P) at (0,1.5);
\coordinate (Q) at (1.5,3);
\coordinate (R) at (4,3);
\coordinate (S) at (5.5,1.5);
\coordinate (T) at (4,0);
\coordinate (U) at (1.5,0);
\draw (P)--(Q)--(R)--(S)--(T)--(U)--(P);
\draw (P)..controls (-1.2,2.5) and (-1.2,0.5)..(P);
\draw (Q) to[bend left=30] (R);
\draw (Q)--(U);
\draw (R)--(T);
\foreach \v/\pos in {P/above left,Q/above left,R/above right,S/right,T/below,U/below}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);\path (\v) node[\pos] {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Six vertices require five edges. First remove the loop at $P$ and the curved duplicate edge $QR$.

For the first tree, remove $QU$, $RT$ and $UP$, each from a cycle. All vertices remain connected along $P-Q-R-S-T-U$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\coordinate (P) at (0,1.5);
\coordinate (Q) at (1.5,3);
\coordinate (R) at (4,3);
\coordinate (S) at (5.5,1.5);
\coordinate (T) at (4,0);
\coordinate (U) at (1.5,0);
\draw (P)--(Q)--(R)--(S)--(T)--(U);
\foreach \v/\pos in {P/left,Q/above,R/above,S/right,T/below,U/below}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);\path (\v) node[\pos] {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

For the second tree, instead remove $PQ$, $ST$ and $TU$. The remaining edges are $PU$, $UQ$, $QR$, $RS$ and $RT$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\coordinate (P) at (0,1.5);
\coordinate (Q) at (1.5,3);
\coordinate (R) at (4,3);
\coordinate (S) at (5.5,1.5);
\coordinate (T) at (4,0);
\coordinate (U) at (1.5,0);
\draw (P)--(U)--(Q)--(R)--(S);
\draw (R)--(T);
\foreach \v/\pos in {P/left,Q/above,R/above,S/right,T/below,U/below}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);\path (\v) node[\pos] {$\v$};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Each drawing retains all six original vertices, uses five original edges and has no cycles. Other spanning trees are possible.
